<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d6" for="edge" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="edge" attr.name="keywords" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="edge" attr.name="description" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d3" for="edge" attr.name="weight" attr.type="double" />
  <key id="d2" for="node" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="node" attr.name="description" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="node" attr.name="entity_type" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;BANKSIA LEMANNIANA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Banksia lemanniana is a species of woody shrub of the family Proteaceae native to Western Australia, known for its unique characteristics such as stiff serrated leaves and unusual hanging inflorescences."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW LANTERN BANKSIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ALIAS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Yellow lantern banksia is an alternative name for Banksia lemanniana."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEMANN'S BANKSIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ALIAS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lemann's banksia is another name for Banksia lemanniana, often used in botanical contexts."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RED LANTERN BANKSIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ALIAS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Red lantern banksia is an alternative name for Banksia lemanniana, sometimes used to distinguish it from other varieties."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WESTERN AUSTRALIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Western Australia is the state where Banksia lemanniana is native and where it grows within and just east of the Fitzgerald River National Park."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FITZGERALD RIVER NATIONAL PARK&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fitzgerald River National Park is a specific area in Western Australia where Banksia lemanniana can be found."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Proteaceae is the family to which Banksia lemanniana belongs, characterized by unique features such as hanging inflorescences."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BUSHFIRE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bushfire is a natural disaster that can kill Banksia lemanniana, but it also regenerates from seed after such events."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GREENISH BUDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Greenish buds are a stage in the flowering process of Banksia lemanniana, developing into oval flower spikes before turning grey."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OVAL FLOWER SPIKES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oval flower spikes are a distinctive feature of Banksia lemanniana, developing from greenish buds and turning grey."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARGE WOODY FOLLICLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Large woody follicles are the characteristic fruiting structures of Banksia lemanniana, developing from the grey flower spikes."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="&quot;BANKSIA LEMANNIANA&quot;" target="&quot;YELLOW LANTERN BANKSIA&quot;">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Banksia lemanniana is known by the alias 'yellow lantern banksia'."</data>
      <data key="d5">"alias relationship"</data>
      <data key="d6">ramdocs-0092-abd8ca5b1126</data>
    </edge>
